course: dictionary
lesson: 7678
title: Prosecute: taking someone to court
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Prosecute» is a verb, an action word. In a court of law, to prosecute someone means to officially accuse them of a crime and try to prove they are guilty. The clue is in the sentence: a sign that says «Trespassers will be prosecuted» warns that people who go onto private land can be taken to court.
explain: Read the words around it to work out which meaning is right. A history book might say a country «prosecuted the war». A war cannot go to court, so prosecute must mean something else: to carry on with something until it is finished.
explain: Check by swapping in a meaning. In the tale, a dog says, «I'll be judge, I'll be jury», which means one side would decide everything, a very unfair trial. The word prosecute in that story still means take someone to court, so the swap makes sense.
fact: Prosecute is a verb: prosecutes, prosecuted and prosecuting are its other forms.
fact: In law, the people who prosecute are the prosecution, often the police or the state.
fact: The defence argues for the person accused, and the judge or jury decides.
fact: «Prosecute» can also mean to carry on with something, such as a war or a plan, until it is finished.
fact: After England lost the Euro 2020 final on penalties, some people who abused players online were prosecuted; the right move with a hateful post is to report it, not share it.
exercise: «Trespassers will be prosecuted.» What does prosecuted mean on this sign? | *Warned politely to say sorry | Let into the garden | Given a free cup of tea | Taken to court and accused of a crime
exercise: A history book says a country «prosecuted the war». What does prosecuted mean here? | Wrote a poem about the war | *Carried on with the war until it was finished | Stopped the war at once
exercise: Some people who posted hateful messages online were prosecuted. What is the right move with a hateful post? | Copy it into a new post | *Report it | Share it with your friends | Reply with more hate
test: «The police decided to prosecute the man for stealing the bike.» What does prosecute mean here? | To lend him a bike for the day | *To take him to court to prove he is guilty | To give him a prize for his bike | To say sorry to him on behalf of the shop
test: Which word could replace prosecute in «They decided to prosecute the plan until it was finished»? | Forget | Carry on with | Stop | *Carry on with, the same as prosecute in its second meaning
exam: In a court of law, who decides whether an accused person is guilty? | The defence lawyer | The crowd outside the court | The person who was accused | *The judge or jury
